    levels: {
      teen: 'Here is something true about every building that has ever existed. It is falling apart right now. Slowly, but right now. Paint gives up. Seals dry out. Water finds the one gap. Metal rusts. Nobody has to do anything wrong for that to happen. It is just what time does to things. The Bible says it straight out: "By much slothfulness the building decayeth; and through idleness of the hands the house droppeth through" (Ecclesiastes 10:18). Droppeth through. That is a roof caving in, described in four words. Now here is the part people get wrong. They think not fixing something is the free option. It is not. Look at the other verse in that same chapter: "If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength" (Ecclesiastes 10:10). A dull axe does not mean less chopping. It means the SAME chopping, and now it is harder, and it takes longer, and you are more likely to get hurt doing it. That is exactly what deferred repairs do. A twenty dollar seal today is a two thousand dollar floor next year. Nobody chose the two thousand dollars. They just kept not choosing the twenty. So here is the habit that actually beats this, and it is boring on purpose. Pick the systems that can ruin a building if they fail. Roof. Heat. Water heater. Gutters. Smoke alarms. For each one, write down the last date it was actually serviced. Not “recently.” Not “I think we did that.” A date. Any blank you cannot fill in is not a hole in your paperwork. It is a hole in the building. And then put the next date on a calendar before you close the notebook. That is the whole system. It is not clever and it is not hard. The reason it works is that it does not wait for anybody to notice a problem. Because the slow part — the decaying part — is silent. That is what makes it dangerous. By the time something is loud enough to complain about, you are already paying the expensive version.',
      senior: 'Open the maintenance course with what is arguably the most useful physical observation in Scripture, and teach it as the law it is. "By much slothfulness the building decayeth; and through idleness of the hands the house droppeth through" (Ecclesiastes 10:18). Note the two verbs, because they are not synonyms. DECAYETH is gradual and largely invisible. DROPPETH THROUGH is the failure event, and it is what decay becomes when it is left alone long enough. The verse is describing a rate, not a state, and the moral cause is named plainly rather than blamed on circumstance. Then set the second verse beside it, because it answers the objection every budget meeting raises. "If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength: but wisdom is profitable to direct" (Ecclesiastes 10:10). The postponed maintenance is not cancelled work. It is the same work, later, harder, and paid for at a worse price — plus the collateral it took on the way down. Teach the practical form directly, since this is the lesson everything else in the course rests on. Maintenance is not a project with a completion date. It is a RATE that must at least match the rate of decay, or the building loses ground every year no matter how energetic any single repair season looks. And name the failure mode honestly, because it is almost never a decision. Nobody resolves to let a roof go. They simply never schedule it, and the absence of a schedule is the decision. The instrument, then, is a written service interval for each system with a real date beside it — not a memory, not an intention, and not a reaction to the first leak. Close by naming the second-order effect, because it is what turns a maintenance failure into a business failure. Deferred work does not only cost more later; it removes options. A roof serviced on schedule is a planned expense with three competing quotes and a chosen date. The same roof after it fails is an emergency, purchased at whatever rate is available on the day, from whoever answers, with occupants displaced and no leverage whatsoever on price or quality. The deferral converted a decision into a reaction, and reactions are always bought at retail. That is the real content of “wisdom is profitable to direct” — direction implies a choice still exists, and the entire purpose of maintaining on an interval is to keep the choice.',
    },
    lesson: 'This course opens on a fact about matter that Scripture states more precisely than most maintenance manuals do. "By much slothfulness the building decayeth; and through idleness of the hands the house droppeth through" (Ecclesiastes 10:18). Read the two verbs separately, because they describe two different stages of one process. DECAYETH is the slow part — the seal hardening, the flashing lifting a sixteenth of an inch, the grout letting one drop through per rain. It is gradual, it is nearly invisible, and it is happening on every property you own at this moment. DROPPETH THROUGH is the other end of the same line: the ceiling that comes down, the floor that gives, the failure that finally announces itself. The verse is telling you these are not two different problems. They are the same problem at two different times, and the only variable between them is how long nobody looked. NOW THE OBJECTION, which is the real one and deserves a real answer: money is finite and everything cannot be done at once. Scripture answers it in the same chapter. "If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength: but wisdom is profitable to direct" (Ecclesiastes 10:10). Consider what that verse is actually claiming. The man who does not sharpen has not avoided any work. The tree still has to come down. What he has done is convert a two-minute task into an hour of additional swinging, plus the fatigue, plus the elevated chance of an injury from a tool that skids instead of bites. THIS IS THE ARITHMETIC OF DEFERRED MAINTENANCE and it runs in exactly one direction. A failed roof seal is a repair. The same seal a year on is a repair plus drywall. Two years on it is a repair plus drywall plus framing plus, in the bad cases, the remediation and the tenant relocation and the claim. Nobody ever decided to spend the larger number. They simply declined the smaller one, repeatedly, and each individual declination was defensible. SO NAME THE FAILURE MODE ACCURATELY, because it is not laziness in the way the word "slothfulness" sounds to a modern ear. Almost no one resolves to let a building deteriorate. What happens is that maintenance has no owner, no calendar and no line in the budget, so it is performed only in response to complaints — and a complaint-driven system by construction never catches anything until after it has already become the expensive version. THE PRINCIPLE THE WHOLE COURSE RESTS ON: maintenance is a RATE, not a project. Decay runs continuously, so the only thing that holds a building level is spending that also runs continuously, at least as fast. A property with an energetic renovation every eight years and nothing in between is not maintained; it is alternately neglected and rescued, and it costs far more than the same building serviced on an interval. "Wisdom is profitable to direct" — direct, meaning aimed in advance, at what has not broken yet.',
