    bigIdea: 'This proverb answers the manager who is proud of a low expense line. "Where no oxen are, the crib is clean: but much increase is by the strength of the ox" (Proverbs 14:4). A clean stall is not an accomplishment. It is the evidence that nothing is in it — nothing eating, nothing making a mess, and nothing PRODUCING. The mess and the increase come from the same animal, and you cannot keep one without the other. Then the companion line divides two ways of spending a day: "In all labour there is profit: but the talk of the lips tendeth only to penury" (Proverbs 14:23). The upkeep an asset demands is not a leak. It is the price of the increase.',
    inApp: 'For one property, list what it COSTS you each year to keep in service — maintenance, reserves, tools, the trades, the hours. Then list what it produced. Most owners can recite the first list from memory and have to go and find the second. Put them side by side on one page, because the proverb’s whole point is that neither number means anything on its own.',
