      senior: 'Teach Proverbs 14:4 as the corrective to a specific and very common management error: treating a low expense line as evidence of good stewardship. "Where no oxen are, the crib is clean: but much increase is by the strength of the ox." The crib is the feeding trough. The observation is deliberately deflating: a spotless stall is not an accomplishment, it is a diagnosis — the stall is empty. And the second clause supplies the reason the mess is tolerable. The increase and the mess proceed from the SAME SOURCE. The ox that fouls the stall is the ox that ploughs the field, and the two cannot be separated by any arrangement of will or policy. Now transfer it, because the error it corrects is everywhere in property. A maintenance line that came in remarkably low is an ambiguous signal, and it is routinely read as though it were a clear one. It can mean genuine efficiency — good systems, good intervals, good trades. Or it can mean nothing was serviced, in which case the expense was not avoided but merely DEFERRED into a future period, usually with interest and collateral attached. The two possibilities are indistinguishable from the expense line alone, which is exactly why it must never be read alone. The same logic governs every productive asset a business holds. A vehicle with no service history is not an inexpensive vehicle; it is an unrealised breakdown. Tooling that is never sharpened or replaced is not a saving; it is slower work, worse output, and — per the blunt-iron verse of lesson one — more strength required for the same result. A skilled trade relationship that is never paid promptly is not a margin; it is a phone that stops being answered. So the instrument is a paired reading, always, and never a single number. Set what an asset COSTS beside what it PRODUCED, on one page, for the same period. Most owners can recite the cost from memory and must go and look up the production, which is itself the finding. And close with the companion verse, because it draws the line between two ways a day can be spent. "In all labour there is profit: but the talk of the lips tendeth only to penury" (Proverbs 14:23). Deliberation is not maintenance. A roof discussed for four quarters is a roof that has been failing for four quarters, and the deliberation has not contributed a single shingle.',
