    lesson: 'This proverb is aimed precisely at the manager who is proud of a low expense line, and it is deflating on purpose. "Where no oxen are, the crib is clean: but much increase is by the strength of the ox" (Proverbs 14:4). The crib is the feeding trough in a stall. So picture the spotless barn — no scattered straw, nothing to muck out, no feed bill, no mess whatsoever. Solomon’s response is not admiration. It is a diagnosis: of course it is clean, THERE IS NO OX IN IT. And the second clause tells you why the mess is worth tolerating. The increase and the mess proceed from the SAME SOURCE. The animal that fouls the stall every day is the identical animal that pulls the plough, and no arrangement of policy, discipline or good intentions can separate the two. You may have both or neither. NOW TRANSFER IT, because the error this verse corrects is endemic in property management. A maintenance line that came in remarkably low is an AMBIGUOUS signal, and it is routinely read as though it were a clear one. It can mean genuine efficiency — sound systems, honest service intervals, good trades who do not come back twice for the same fault. Or it can mean that nothing was serviced at all, in which case the expense was not avoided but simply relocated into a future period, generally with collateral damage attached and always at a worse rate. Those two possibilities produce the identical number on the page. They are indistinguishable from the expense line alone, which is the precise reason that line must never be read alone. THE SAME LOGIC GOVERNS EVERY PRODUCTIVE ASSET A BUSINESS HOLDS, and it is worth naming several so the pattern becomes visible. A vehicle with no service history is not an inexpensive vehicle; it is a breakdown that has not happened yet, and it will happen at the least convenient hour available. Tooling that is never sharpened or replaced is not a saving; it is slower work, poorer output, and — exactly as the blunt-iron verse in lesson one said — more strength required to achieve the same result. A skilled trade relationship that is never paid promptly is not an improvement in margin; it is a phone that gradually stops being answered, which costs far more than the float ever earned. SO THE INSTRUMENT IS A PAIRED READING, ALWAYS, and never a single number. Set what an asset COSTS beside what it PRODUCED, on one page, over the same period. Do it for the property, for the vehicle, for the tooling, for the trade relationship. And notice what almost always happens when an owner attempts this for the first time: he can recite the cost from memory and has to go and look up the production. That asymmetry is itself the finding, and it explains a great deal about why cost gets cut before output gets measured. AND THE COMPANION VERSE CLOSES IT, because it divides the two ways a working day can be spent. "In all labour there is profit: but the talk of the lips tendeth only to penury" (Proverbs 14:23). Labour produces. Discussion of labour does not, and the distinction matters more in maintenance than almost anywhere else, because maintenance is uniquely easy to deliberate about indefinitely. A roof that has been discussed for four quarters is a roof that has been FAILING for four quarters, and not one of those meetings contributed a single shingle to it.',
