    lesson: 'The course closes on a name, and it is a name Yahweh hands out to people who repair things. "And they that shall be of thee shall build the old waste places: thou shalt raise up the foundations of many generations; and thou shalt be called, The repairer of the breach, The restorer of paths to dwell in" (Isaiah 58:12). TAKE THE THREE ELEMENTS IN ORDER, because each one adds something the previous did not. OLD WASTE PLACES BUILT — note that this is not new construction on clean ground. It is the recovery of what was already lost, which is harder, considerably less celebrated, and precisely what maintenance is. Nobody photographs a roof that did not fail. FOUNDATIONS OF MANY GENERATIONS RAISED — the work is designed to outlast the worker, which means it must be assessed on a horizon longer than any single owner’s tenure. A man who maintains only to the end of his own holding period is not raising foundations; he is managing an exit. AND THEN THE TITLES THEMSELVES, which repay slow reading. THE REPAIRER OF THE BREACH: a breach is the failure point in a defensive wall — the specific place through which everything unwanted came in. To be named its repairer is to be named the one who closed the way harm was arriving. THE RESTORER OF PATHS TO DWELL IN: not merely passable paths, but habitable ones. The standard is not function; it is fitness for someone to live there. NOW THE MOVE THAT MAKES THIS THE RIGHT CLOSING LESSON, and it is the chapter’s own move rather than one we are importing. Isaiah 58 does not attach that name to technical competence. It attaches it to the fast that Yahweh actually chooses, and the chapter defines that fast in strictly material terms: "Is it not to deal thy bread to the hungry, and that thou bring the poor that are cast out to thy house?" (Isaiah 58:7). Bread handed to the hungry. The dispossessed brought INTO a house. And out of that conduct — not out of skill, not out of licensure, not out of portfolio size — comes the repairer title. SO THE CHAPTER REFUSES A SEPARATION THAT OUR OWN VOCABULARY KEEPS MAKING: between the building as an asset and the people the building exists for. And that refusal is not a devotional flourish appended to a practical course. It is the thing that makes all seven preceding lessons cohere into one argument. The decay in lesson one is a roof over somebody. The untempered morter in lesson three is a wall somebody stands under and does not know is hollow. The trust in lesson four is what gets a man out of bed at four in the morning when a pipe opens above a sleeping family. The bag with holes in lesson six is money that could have gone into a repair somebody actually needed. WHY THIS LESSON COMES LAST: because the preceding seven are all technique, and technique without this is merely competent asset management, which the world already teaches adequately and does not need us for. Strictly speaking there is no such thing as maintaining property. There is keeping a roof over a person, and Yahweh named that work Himself — twice, in a single verse, and in language He does not spend lightly. THE HONEST CLOSING QUESTION FOR ANY STEWARD is therefore not whether the portfolio performed this year. It is whether the old waste places are being built, whether the foundations being laid will outlast the man laying them, and whether the paths being restored are ones somebody can actually dwell on.',
